# A review policy tested with the necessity criterion.
#
# Sixteen review findings about one paper, labelled by the decision they received:
#   objects 0-9  adopted   (a gap sentence in the abstract, an error base, a claim tightened
#                          from "proved" to "shown", a theorem reference instead of a literal
#                          range, two cross-reference fixes, cost units, a percentage corrected
#                          from "a few" to 15%, a section title, a figure caption)
#   objects 10-15 rejected (a wholesale tense rewrite, a section reorder, a voice rewrite,
#                          seeds without data, percentage bases without data, timings without
#                          a machine)
#
# The first vocabulary is the audit's own taxonomy: which part of the paper a finding touches.
# It cannot express the policy. Objects 1 and 13 share every category predicate (both touch a
# number) but received opposite decisions, and so do 2 and 10 (both touch wording), and 2 and 12.
# The state is therefore exhausted, exactly as Theorem 2 says: no consistent rule over these
# predicates exists, and no re-reading of the same categories will produce one.

objects 16
add f_abstract 0
add f_titles 8
add f_captions 9
add f_structure 11
add f_numbers 1 6 7 13 14 15
add f_voice 2 10 12
add f_xref 3 4 5

vote 0 3 0
vote 1 3 0
vote 2 3 0
vote 3 3 0
vote 4 3 0
vote 5 3 0
vote 6 3 0
vote 7 3 0
vote 8 3 0
vote 9 3 0
vote 10 0 3
vote 11 0 3
vote 12 0 3
vote 13 0 3
vote 14 0 3
vote 15 0 3

need            # expect INSUFFICIENT / PROPOSE, colliding pairs (1,13) (6,14) (7,15) (2,10) (2,12)

# The missing predicates are not categories but costs: whether the fix needs a measurement the
# paper does not have, whether it disturbs the layout, whether it is a matter of taste.
add needs_data 13 14 15
add layout_risk 11
add taste 10 12

need            # expect IDENTIFIED / STOP, colliding pairs none
